    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "it doesn't feel like regular RFI though. the pattern is too rhythmic, like it's reacting to when i'm actually in the room or when i'm on my phone. and those pressure shifts on the 17th were so localized... it felt like the air was literally vibrating with information. if someone wanted to use something like VLF (very low frequency) or even infrasound to broadcast data without being detected by standard radio sweeps, could they theoretically use those atmospheric pressure pockets to 'guide' a signal? it just seems like a way to hide a transmission in plain sight.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "While VLF and infrasound can theoretically travel long distances through the atmosphere, they are too weak to carry complex data streams just by \"guiding\" random pressure pockets; there must be a deliberate, modulated transmitter. The rhythmic nature you describe sounds much more like electromagnetic interference from your phone or house wiring interacting with the air, a phenomenon known as common-mode noise, rather than a hidden broadcast. Even if someone tried to hide a signal using atmospheric resonance, it would require incredibly precise equipment and wouldn't exploit natural weather pressure shifts as a single-use conduit for information.",
      "source": "target",
